-- Dialy: store food names in both German variants instead of converting
-- them in the app:
--   name             Swiss Standard German  "Schlagrahm", "Pouletbrust", "Glace",
--                                           "Rüebli", "ss" ("Griess", "süss")
--   names['de-DE']   German Standard German "Schlagsahne", "Hähnchenbrust", "Eis",
--                                           "Karotten", "ß" ("Grieß", "süß")
-- The app shows names['de-DE'] in "Deutsch (Deutschland)" and `name` in
-- "Deutsch (Schweiz)"; search finds both. 'de-DE' is only stored where it
-- differs from the Swiss name, like the en/fr/it translations.
--
-- The source data is mixed (BLV is Swiss, the BLS and many Open Food Facts
-- products German), so 1879 names become Swiss ("Schlagsahne" ->
-- "Schlagrahm", "ß" -> "ss") and 3229 foods get a 'de-DE' name. The words
-- come from scripts/lib/germanVariants.mjs, which the Dialy-Editor also uses
-- when it transfers foods; ambiguous terms (Peperoni, "Plätzli" for patties)
-- are left alone. Names can be corrected in the Dialy-Editor ("Name in
-- Deutschland").
--
-- Only curated and imported foods are touched, not user-owned ones. A row is
-- only touched if its name is still the listed one, so later edits in the
-- Dialy-Editor are not overwritten. The foods_touch_updated_at trigger (0010)
-- bumps updated_at, so the app picks the changes up on its next sync.

update public.foods f
set
  name = v.new_name,
  names = case
    when v.name_de is null then f.names - 'de-DE'
    else f.names || jsonb_build_object('de-DE', v.name_de)
  end
from (values
  ('food-weissbrot', 'Weissbrot (Ruchbrot)', 'Weissbrot (Ruchbrot)', 'Weißbrot (Ruchbrot)'),
  ('food-poulet', 'Pouletbrust', 'Pouletbrust', 'Hähnchenbrust'),
